@only-if workflows
# __NAME__ infrastructure

Two Terraform roots: `foundation` (slow, shared resources) and `instance` (per-deployment resources). See
`azure:structure` for why they're split. Both are driven by the app profile at `infra/app.json`, written by
the app stack; `azure:scaffold` turns it into the workload, host and database resources it asks for. Run
`python -B <scaffold-skill-dir>/scripts/scaffold.py options --schema` to see azure's own, app-agnostic
options as JSON.

## Bootstrap

Foundation starts with local state, using a personal Azure account kept isolated from any other CLI session:

```sh
export AZURE_CONFIG_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
az login
cd infra/foundation
terraform init
terraform apply -var-file=../env/ci.tfvars
```

Then migrate into the state storage account `apply` just created:

```sh
cat > backend.auto.tfvars.json <<JSON
{}
JSON
terraform init -migrate-state \
  -backend-config="resource_group_name=$(terraform output -raw resource_group_name)" \
  -backend-config="storage_account_name=$(terraform output -raw state_storage_account_name)" \
  -backend-config="container_name=tfstate" \
  -backend-config="key=__NAME__/foundation.tfstate"
```

`instance` uses the same account with key `__NAME__/instance.tfstate`, or
`__NAME__/instance.<ephemeral_id>.tfstate` for a PR's preview instance.

`infra/.gitignore` excludes `.terraform/`, state, plan and crash files and any `override.tf*`; it does not
ignore `.terraform.lock.hcl`, which `azure:build` requires to be committed.

## GitHub environments and repository variables

Create three GitHub environments: `ci` (the shared non-production environment `ci.yml` deploys), `preview`
(a pull request's ephemeral instance) and `prod`. Give `preview` and `prod` protection rules in repository
settings â€” at minimum, restrict `prod` to deployments from tags matching `v[0-9]+.[0-9]+.[0-9]+`; a preview
or production deploy should not run unreviewed.

`prod.yml` triggers only on a semver tag push (`v[0-9]+.[0-9]+.[0-9]+`) and its first job,
`assert-release-commit`, fails the run unless that tag's commit is the head of the latest successful
`ci.yml` run on `main` â€” a release always ships what CI last verified, never an untested commit.

`pr.yml`, `ci.yml`, `prod.yml`, `pr-closed.yml`, `deploy-environment.yml` and `destroy-preview.yml` read
these GitHub Actions variables, set once the foundation has deployed:

- `AZURE_FOUNDATION_READY` â€” `"true"` once the state account above exists; gates PR plan, build, preview and recovery. CI can converge its bootstrapped foundation.
- `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` â€” the deploy identity's client id (`terraform output -raw deploy_identity_client_id`).
- `AZURE_TENANT_ID`, `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` â€” the target tenant and subscription.
- `TFSTATE_RESOURCE_GROUP`, `TFSTATE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT` â€” the values used in the migration above.

Set these on the `ci` environment, and again on `prod` with that environment's own subscription and storage
account. A PR's preview instance deploys and destroys through the `preview` environment's values, which
should match `ci`'s, since a preview reuses `ci`'s foundation â€” only its `instance` root is ephemeral. The
`ci` foundation's deploy identity carries a federated credential for both the `environment:ci` and
`environment:preview` OIDC subjects, so the same identity can sign in under either GitHub environment.

A pull request's preview is opt-in: add the `deploy-preview` label to the PR to deploy it, and `pr-closed.yml`
checks for and destroys it on close, including after that label has been removed. Applying a label requires the repository's `triage` role,
which is what makes the label a safe trust gate â€” a pull request from a fork can't grant itself a preview
deploy by labelling its own PR. `plan`, `deploy-preview`, the state-lock release and `destroy-preview` also
check `github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository` as defense in depth.

Update `infra/env/ci.tfvars` (and `infra/env/prod.tfvars`) with the real GitHub owner, repository and
their numeric ids:

```sh
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo> --jq '.owner.id, .id'
```

The first line is `github_owner_id`, the second is `github_repository_id`.

The federated credentials expect GitHub's immutable OIDC subject, `repo:<owner>@<owner-id>/<repo>@<repo-id>:...`,
which repositories created after 2026-07-15 send by default. Opt an older repository into immutable subjects
in its Actions OIDC settings before the first plan, or no login will match.

`database.schemas` (default `["public"]`) lists every Postgres schema a migration needs; the migration job
creates each non-`public` one and grants every workload identity the same privileges on it as on `public`.

An App Service workload's `sticky` list names its own effective settings that should stay with the production
slot across a swap, instead of swapping along with the rest of `app_settings`.

## Build once and deploy the verified artifact

`github-actions:scaffold` owns the workflow graph and action catalog. Azure supplies its provider phases,
`infra/deployment.json` and `infra/scripts/azure_deploy.py`. The shared writer uploads the build artifact
once and downloads the same immutable artifact ID for CI, preview and production deployment.

The build phase runs each selected `build.image` or `build.package` command from the profile. Image
commands receive `REGISTRY`, `REPOSITORY`, `TAG` and `IMAGE`; they must push `$IMAGE` to the CI registry.
Azure reads that image's matching `RepoDigests`, records its identity/platform and saves a single-platform
image archive. Package commands receive `PACKAGE`, the zip path to write. The manifest records source
commit/run, workload host, safe relative filename and SHA-256 for each asset. The database migration asset
is `database-migration`, independent of workloads named `migration` or `migrator`.

CI builds can converge the CI foundation; PR builds only read the preprovisioned CI foundation. Foundation
bootstrapping and `AZURE_FOUNDATION_READY` still require the setup above. A foundation-only profile has no
build artifact.

Deploy validates all manifest source/workload/host/digest/path/hash data before any instance apply. It
converges foundation for CI or production and reuses CI foundation for a preview. Production loads image
archives and pushes that exact content to its own registry, checking that the digest is unchanged. It
needs no CI-registry access and does not rerun build commands. The instance and migration consume only
target-registry digest references.

Resource commands use Terraform's actual `workload_targets`, `resource_group_name` and
`migration_job_name` outputs. Migration waits for the exact started execution. Packages deploy to the
actual Function App or App Service staging slot; App Service must pass staging health before a swap and
production health afterward. Container Apps must serve the latest active/Running/Healthy revision with
the promoted image digest, and HTTP workloads must route traffic to it and pass their serving probe.
Background workloads require the same current revision and image health. Flex Consumption must be Running
and answer from the actual serving host. Every readiness wait fails after five minutes.

The full `PREVIEW_NUMBER` becomes `pr<N>`; identities beyond six characters fail instead of being
truncated. Destroy queries that exact state blob before Terraform initialization and is idempotent when
it is absent. Recovery does nothing for absent/unleased state and breaks only that exact leased blob.
Query/authentication failures fail the job. Foundation is never destroyed by preview cleanup. The shared
writer owns the held preview lock, live PR-state checks and cleanup from the current default branch.

Ordered `profile.verify` commands become unit verification steps; an absent or empty list emits no app
verification suite. SDK setup comes from repository metadata files rather than Azure's config.

## Offline gate

Run this for each root before pushing, and in CI without any Azure login:

```sh
terraform fmt -check -recursive
terraform init -backend=false
terraform validate
```
